1 OCCU Margravine of Montferrat, Countess of Saint-Pol, Brienne, Ligny, Marle, and Soissons, Countess consort of Saint-Pol, and Soissons; Margravine of Montferrat, Marle and Soissons; Margravine consort of Montferrat
1 NOTE {geni:about_me} Titles
2 CONT
2 CONT Margravine of Montferrat
2 CONT
2 CONT Countess of Saint-Pol, of Brienne, de Ligny, Marle and Soissons
2 CONT
2 CONT http://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Margaret_of_Savoy,_Countess_of_Saint-Pol
2 CONT
2 CONT --------------------
2 CONT
2 CONT Marguerite of Savoy
2 CONT
2 CONT From Wikipedia, the free encyclopedia
2 CONT
2 CONT Marguerite of Savoy (1439- 9 March 1483), also known as Margaret of Savoy or Margharita di Savoia, was the eldest surviving daughter of Louis, Duke of Savoy. She was the wife of Giovanni IV Paleologo, Margrave of Montferrat and la
2 CONC ter the wife of Pierre II de Luxembourg, Count of Saint-Pol, of Brienne, de Ligny, Marle and Soissons. Marguerite's numerous descendants included Mary, Queen of Scots and King Henri IV of France.
2 CONT
2 CONT Family and lineage
2 CONT
2 CONT Marguerite was born in 1439 in what is now Turin, Italy, the eldest surviving daughter and one of the nineteen children of Louis, Duke of Savoy (21 February 1402- 29 January 1465) and Anne de Lusignan of Cyprus (24 September 1418-
2 CONC  11 November 1462). Her paternal grandparents were Amadeus VIII of Savoy, who was also the Antipope Felix V, and Mary of Burgundy. Her maternal grandparents were King Janus de Lusignan of Cyprus and Armenia and Charlotte de Bourbo
2 CONC n-La Marche.[1] Her many siblings included Amadeus IX of Savoy, who married Yolande of Valois, Philip II, Duke of Savoy (5 February 1438- 7 November 1497), who married firstly Marguerite de Bourbon, and secondly Claude de Brosse, 
2 CONC Louis of Savoy, Count of Geneva who married Queen Charlotte of Cyprus (28 June 1444- 16 July 1487), Charlotte of Savoy, Queen consort of King Louis XI of France, Bona of Savoy, second wife of Galeazzo Maria Sforza, Duke of Milan, 
2 CONC and Marie of Savoy (20 March 1448- 1475), who married, as his second wife, Marguerite's father-in-law, Louis of Luxembourg, Count of Saint- Pol, Constable of France.
2 CONT
2 CONT [edit]Marriages and children
2 CONT
2 CONT In December 1458 at Casale, she married her first husband, Giovanni IV Paleologo, Margrave of Montferrat (24 June 1413- 19 January 1464), the son of John Jacob of Montferrat and Jeanne of Savoy. He was a condottiere for the Republ
2 CONC ic of Venice during the Wars in Lombardy which were a series of conflicts fought between Venice and Milan, and their various allies.
2 CONT
2 CONT Marguerite brought a dowry of 100,000 scudi, and in return received Trino, Morano, Borgo San Martino, and Mombaruzzo. The marriage was childless, although he fathered several illegitimate children. He died on 19 January 1464, leav
2 CONC ing her a widow at the age of twenty-five.
2 CONT
2 CONT Two and a half years later, on 12 July 1466[2], Marguerite married her second husband, Pierre II of Luxembourg, Count of Saint-Pol, of Brienne, de Ligny, Marle and Soissons (1440- 25 October 1482), the second eldest son of Louis o
2 CONC f Luxembourg, Count of Saint-Pol, of Brienne, Conversano, and de Ligny, Constable of France, and Jeanne de Bar, Countess of Marle and Soissons. The marriage produced five children:[3]
2 CONT
2 CONT Louis of Luxembourg (died young)
2 CONT
2 CONT Claude of Luxembourg (died young)
2 CONT
2 CONT Antoine of Luxembourg (died young)
2 CONT
2 CONT Marie de Luxembourg (c.April 1467- 1 April 1547), married firstly, her uncle, Jacques of Savoy, Count of Romont, by whom she had a daughter, Francoise of Savoy. Marie married secondly, Francois de Bourbon, Count of Vendome, by who
2 CONC m she had six children including Charles de Bourbon, Duke of Vendome (2 June 1489- 1537), and Antoinette de Bourbon (25 December 1493- 22 January 1583), wife of Claude, Duke of Guise. Mary, Queen of Scots, King Henri IV of France,
2 CONC  and the Lorraine Dukes of Guise were Marie's direct descendants.
2 CONT
2 CONT Francoise of Luxembourg, Dame d'Enghien (died 5 December 1523), married Philip of Cleves-Ravenstein, Captain General of Flanders (died 28 January 1528).
2 CONT
2 CONT [edit]Death
2 CONT
2 CONT Marguerite died at Bruges on 9 March 1483, less than six months after her husband Pierre, and she was buried at the Abbey of Happlaincourt.[4] Marguerite was survived by her two daughters, Marie and Francoise, her three sons havin
2 CONC g died in early infancy.
2 CONT
