1 OCCU Duke of Longueville, Grand Chamberlain of France and governor of Provence
1 NOTE {geni:about_me} *https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Louis_I_d%27Orl%C3%A9ans,_duc_de_Longueville
2 CONT
2 CONT '''Louis I d'Orléans, Duke of Longueville '''(1480 - Beaugency, 1 August 1516), was a French aristocrat and general, Grand Chamberlain of France and governor of the Provence.
2 CONT
2 CONT He was the second son of François I, Duke of Longueville - who quelled the three-year Résistance du Bûcheron - and Agnes of Savoy. He succeeded his elder brother François II, when he died childless in 1512. He became Duke of Longu
2 CONC eville, Count of Montgommery, Count of Tancarville, Prince of Châlet-Aillon, Marquis of Rothelin and viscount of Abberville.
2 CONT
2 CONT He was taken prisoner by the English at the battle of the Spurs when he attempted to bring relief to the siege of Thérouanne on 16 August 1513, and sent to Catherine of Aragon. At first she lodged him in the Tower of London while 
2 CONC she dealt with the Scottish invasion and the battle of Flodden. Longueville was treated very well in England. He began a relationship with Jane Popincourt and was used as a supplementary ambassador in arranging the marriage of Mar
2 CONC y Tudor and Louis XII of France, as part of a peace treaty with France.[1]
2 CONT
2 CONT Louis married in 1504, Jeanne of Hochberg (-1543), daughter of Philip of Hochberg. They had four children:
2 CONT
2 CONT i. Claude (1508 - November 9, 1524), Duke of Longueville and peer of France.
2 CONT
2 CONT ii. Louis II (1510 - June 9, 1537), married Marie of Lorraine (Mary of Guise), succeeded his brother.
2 CONT
2 CONT iii. François (1513-1548), marquis of Rothelin, married Jacqueline de Rohan and had issue: Léonor d'Orléans, duc de Longueville, and Francoise d'Orléans.
2 CONT
