1 NOTE {geni:about_me} ==Henry II of France==
2 CONT '''Henry II (French: Henri II) (31 March 1519 – 10 July 1559) was a monarch of the House of Valois who ruled as King of France from 31 March 1547 until his death in 1559.[1] The second son''' of Francis I, '''he became Dauphin of 
2 CONC France upon the death of his elder brother''' Francis III, Duke of Brittany, in 1536.
2 CONT https://www.findagrave.com/memorial/12289/henry_ii_of_france
2 CONT '''Henry pursued his father's policies in matter of arts, wars and religion. He persevered in the Italian Wars against the House of Habsburg and tried to suppress the Protestant Reformation even as the Huguenots became an increasi
2 CONC ngly large minority in France during his reign.
2 CONT
2 CONT The Treaty of Cateau-Cambrésis (1559), which put an end to the Italian Wars, had mixed results: France renounced its claims to territories in Italy, but gained certain other territories, including the Pale of Calais and the Three 
2 CONC Bishoprics. France failed to change the balance of power in Europe, as Spain remained the sole dominant power, but it did benefit from the division of the holdings of its ruler, Charles V, and from the weakening of the Holy Roman 
2 CONC Empire, which Charles also ruled.
2 CONT
2 CONT '''Henry suffered an untimely death in a jousting tournament held to celebrate the Peace of Cateau-Cambrésis at the conclusion of the Eighth Italian War. The king's surgeon, Ambroise Paré, was unable to cure the infected wound inf
2 CONC licted by Gabriel de Montgomery, the captain of his Scottish Guard. He was succeeded in turn by three of his sons, whose ineffective reigns helped to spur the ghastly consequences of the French Wars of Religion between Protestants
2 CONC  and Catholics.
2 CONT
2 CONT '''Henry was born in the royal Château de Saint-Germain-en-Laye, near Paris''', the son of Francis I and Claude, Duchess of Brittany (daughter of Louis XII of France and Anne, Duchess of Brittany, and a second cousin of her husban
2 CONC d).
2 CONT
2 CONT His father was captured at the Battle of Pavia in 1525 by the forces of his sworn enemy, Holy Roman Emperor Charles V, and held prisoner in Spain.[2] '''To obtain his release, it was agreed that Henry and his older brother be sent
2 CONC  to Spain in his place.[3] They remained in captivity for over four years.[4]
2 CONT
2 CONT '''Henry married Catherine de' Medici, a member of the ruling family of Florence, on 28 October 1533, when they were both fourteen years old. The following year, he became romantically involved with a thirty-five-year-old widow, D
2 CONC iane de Poitiers. They had always been very close: she had publicly embraced him on the day he set off to Spain, and during a jousting tournament, he insisted that his lance carry her ribbon instead of his wife's. Diane became Hen
2 CONC ry's mistress and most trusted confidante and, for the next twenty-five years, wielded considerable influence behind the scenes, even signing royal documents. Extremely confident, mature and intelligent, she left Catherine powerle
2 CONC ss to intervene.[5] She did, however, insist that Henry sleep with Catherine in order to produce heirs to the throne.[6]
2 CONT
2 CONT When '''his elder brother''' Francis, the Dauphin and Duke of Brittany, died in 1536 after a game of tennis, '''Henry became heir apparent to the throne. He succeeded his father on his 28th birthday and was crowned King of France 
2 CONC on 25 July 1547 at Reims Cathedral.[7]
2 CONT
2 CONT '''Henry's reign was marked by wars with Austria and the persecution of Protestants, mainly Calvinists known as Huguenots. Henry II severely punished them, particularly the ministers, for example by burning at the stake or cutting
2 CONC  off their tongues for uttering heresies. Even those only suspected of being Huguenots could be imprisoned.[citation needed]
2 CONT
2 CONT The Edict of Châteaubriant (27 June 1551) called upon the civil and ecclesiastical courts to detect and punish all heretics and placed severe restrictions on Huguenots, including the loss of one-third of their property to informer
2 CONC s, and confiscations. It also strictly regulated publications by prohibiting the sale, importation or printing of any unapproved book. It was during the reign of Henry II that Huguenot attempts at establishing a colony in Brazil w
2 CONC ere made, with the short-lived formation of France Antarctique.[8]
2 CONT
2 CONT '''The Eighth Italian War of 1551–1559, sometimes known as the Habsburg–Valois War, began when Henry declared war against Holy Roman Emperor Charles V with the intent of recapturing Italy and ensuring French, rather than Habsburg,
2 CONC  domination of European affairs. Persecution of Protestants at home did not prevent Henry II from becoming allied with German Protestant princes at the Treaty of Chambord in 1552. Simultaneously, the continuation of his father's F
2 CONC ranco-Ottoman alliance allowed Henry II to push for French conquests towards the Rhine while a Franco-Ottoman fleet defended southern France.[9] An early offensive into Lorraine was successful. Henry captured the three episcopal c
2 CONC ities of Metz, Toul, and Verdun, and secured them by defeating the Habsburg army at the Battle of Renty in 1554. However the attempted French invasion of Tuscany in 1553 was defeated at the Battle of Marciano.
2 CONT
2 CONT After the abdication of Charles V in 1556, the Habsburg empire was split between Philip II of Spain and Holy Roman Emperor Ferdinand I. '''The focus of Henry's conflict with the Habsburgs shifted to Flanders, where Phillip, in con
2 CONC junction with Emmanuel Philibert of Savoy, defeated the French at the Battle of St. Quentin (1557). England's entry into the war later that year led to the French capture of Calais, and French armies plundered Spanish possessions 
2 CONC in the Low Countries. Henry was nonetheless forced to accept the Peace of Cateau-Cambrésis, in which he renounced any further claims to territories in Italy.[10]
2 CONT
2 CONT '''The Peace of Cateau-Cambrésis was signed between Henry and Elizabeth I of England on 2 April[11] and between Henry and Philip II of Spain on 3 April 1559 at Le Cateau-Cambrésis. Under its terms, France restored Piedmont and Sav
2 CONC oy to the Duke of Savoy, but retained Saluzzo, Calais, and the bishoprics of Metz, Toul, and Verdun. Spain retained Franche-Comté. Emmanuel Philibert, Duke of Savoy, married Margaret of France, Duchess of Berry, '''the sister of H
2 CONC enry II''', and Philip II of Spain married '''Henry's daughter''' Elizabeth of Valois.[12]
2 CONT
2 CONT '''Henry raised''' the young Mary, Queen of Scots, '''at his court, hoping to use her ultimately to establish a dynastic claim to Scotland. On 24 April 1558, Henry's fourteen-year-old son''' Francis was married to Mary in a union 
2 CONC intended to give the future king of France not only the throne of Scotland, but also a claim to the throne of England. '''Henry had Mary sign secret documents, illegal in Scottish law, that would ensure Valois rule in Scotland eve
2 CONC n if she died without an heir'''.[13] Mary's claim to the English throne quickly became an issue when Mary I of England died later in 1558.
2 CONT
2 CONT '''Henry II introduced the concept of publishing the description of an invention in the form of a patent. The idea was to require an inventor to disclose his invention in exchange for monopoly rights to the patent. The description
2 CONC  is called a patent “specification”. The first patent specification was submitted by the inventor Abel Foullon for "Usaige & Description de l'holmetre" (a type of rangefinder). Publication was delayed until after the patent expire
2 CONC d in 1561.[14]
2 CONT
2 CONT '''Henry II was an avid hunter and a participant in jousts and tournaments. On 30 June 1559, at the Place des Vosges at the Hôtel des Tournelles, during a match to celebrate the Peace of Cateau-Cambrésis with his longtime enemies,
2 CONC  the Habsburgs of Austria, and to celebrate the marriage of his daughter''' Elisabeth of Valois to King Philip II of Spain, '''King Henry was wounded in the eye by a fragment of the splintered lance of Gabriel Montgomery, captain 
2 CONC of the King's Scottish Guard.[15] Despite the efforts of royal surgeon Ambroise Paré, the king died of septicemia on 10 July 1559.[16][17] He was buried in a cadaver tomb in Saint Denis Basilica. Henry's death was a factor in the 
2 CONC end of jousting as a sport.[18]
2 CONT
2 CONT '''As Henry lay dying, Queen Catherine limited access to his bedside and denied his mistress Diane de Poitiers permission to see him, even though he repeatedly asked for her. Following his death, Catherine sent Diane into exile, w
2 CONC here she lived in comfort on her own properties until her death.[19]
2 CONT
2 CONT '''It was the practice to enclose the heart of the king in an urn. The Monument to the Heart of Henry II is in the collection of the Louvre, but was originally in the Chapel of Orleans beneath a pyramid. The original bronze urn ho
2 CONC lding the king's heart was destroyed during the French Revolution and a replica was made in the 19th century. The marble sculpture of the Three Graces holding the urn, executed from a single piece of marble by Germain Pilon, the s
2 CONC culptor to Catherine de' Medici, survives.[20]
2 CONT
2 CONT '''Henry was succeeded by his sickly fifteen-year-old son''', Francis II. He was married to sixteen-year-old Mary Queen of Scots, who had been his childhood friend and fiancée since her arrival at the French court when she was fiv
2 CONC e. Francis II died 18 months later in 1560, and Mary returned to Scotland the following summer. Francis II was succeeded by his ten-year-old brother Charles IX. His mother, '''Catherine de Medici''', acted as Regent. Starting in 1
2 CONC 562 and for the forty years following, France was filled with turbulence as Protestants and Catholics fought the bitter Wars of Religion.[15][21]
2 CONT
2 CONT '''Catherine de' Medici bore 10 of Henry's children: (See Children of Henry II of France and Catherine de' Medici)
2 CONT *Francis II, King of France, born 19 January 1544.
2 CONT *Elizabeth of France, born 2 April 1545, married Philip II, King of Spain.
2 CONT *Claude of France, born 12 November 1547, married Charles III, Duke of Lorraine.
2 CONT *Louis, Duke of Orléans, born 3 February 1549, died 24 October 1549.
2 CONT *Charles IX, King of France, born 27 June 1550.
2 CONT *Henry III, King of France, born 19 September 1551, also briefly King of Poland.
2 CONT *Margaret of France, born 14 May 1553, married Henry IV, King of France.
2 CONT *Hercules, Duke of Anjou, born 18 March 1555, later known as Francis, Duke of Alençon and Anjou.
2 CONT *Victoria of France, born 24 June 1556, died 17 August 1556.
2 CONT *Joan of France, stillborn.
2 CONT
2 CONT '''Henry II also had three illegitimate children:
2 CONT
2 CONT '''By Filippa Duci''':[22]
2 CONT *Diane, duchesse d'Angoulême (1538–1619). At the age of fourteen, the younger Diane married Orazio Farnese, Duke of Castro,[23] who died in battle in 1553. Her second marriage was to François, Duke of Montmorency.[24]
2 CONT
2 CONT '''By Lady Janet Stewart''' (1508–1563), the illegitimate daughter of James IV of Scotland:[25]
2 CONT *Henri d'Angoulême (1551 – June 1586). He was legitimized and became governor of Provence.
2 CONT
2 CONT '''By Nicole de Savigny''':[26]
2 CONT *Henri de Saint-Rémy (1557–1621). He was given the title of Count of Saint-Rémy. One of his last descendants was Jeanne de Valois-Saint-Rémy, Countess de la Motte, famous for her role in the Affair of the Diamond Necklace at the c
2 CONC ourt of Louis XVI.
2 CONT
2 CONT Nostradamus (1503–1566), a French apothecary and astrological writer known for his prophecies, is said by most commentators to have become famous when one of his quatrains was construed as '''a prediction of the death of King Henr
2 CONC y II:
2 CONT *CI, Q 35 The young lion shall overcome the older one,
2 CONT *on the field of combat in single battle,
2 CONT *He shall pierce his eyes in a golden cage,
2 CONT *Two forces one, then he shall die a cruel death.
2 CONT
2 CONT But, in fact, the link was first proposed in print only in 1614,[27] fifty-five years after the event and forty-eight after Nostradamus' death; thus it qualifies as a postdiction, or vaticinium ex eventu. The Italian astrologer Lu
2 CONC ca Gaurico, a contemporary of Nostradamus, is also said[by whom?] to have predicted the king's death.
2 CONT
2 CONT '''Henri or Henry has had two notable portrayals''' on the screen. He was played by a young Roger Moore in the 1956 film Diane, opposite Lana Turner in the title role and Marisa Pavan as Catherine de Medici. In the 2013 CW series 
2 CONC Reign he is played by Alan Van Sprang.
2 CONT
2 CONT From: https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Henry_II_of_France
2 CONT
2 CONT _______________________
2 CONT *'''Henri II d' Orleans, King of France, Duke of Brittany & Orleans1,2,3
2 CONT *'''M, #80957, b. 31 March 1519, d. 10 July 1559
2 CONT *Father	François I d' Orleans, King of France, Duke de Valois & Bretagne1 b. 12 Sep 1494, d. 31 Mar 1547
2 CONT *Mother	Claudia of France1 b. 13 Oct 1499, d. 20 Jul 1524
2 CONT *'''     Henri II d' Orleans, King of France, Duke of Brittany & Orleans was born on 31 March 1519 at St. Germaine-en-Laye, France.1 He married Catherine d' Medici''', daughter of Lorenzo II de Medici, Duke d'Urbino and Madeleine 
2 CONC de la Tour, '''on 28 October 1533 at Marseille, Bouche-du-Rhône, Provence-Alpes-Côtes d'Azur, France.1,2,3 Henri II d' Orleans, King of France, Duke of Brittany & Orleans died on 10 July 1559 at Paris, France, at age 40.1
2 CONT *'''Family     Catherine d' Medici''' b. 13 Apr 1519, d. 5 Jan 1589
2 CONT *'''Children	
2 CONT **François II 'le Dauphin', King of France, Dauphin de Vienne1,2,3 b. 19 Jan 1544, d. 5 Dec 1560
2 CONT **Elizabeth of France+1 b. 2 Apr 1545, d. 3 Oct 1568
2 CONT **Claude d' Orleans+1 b. 12 Nov 1547, d. 20 Feb 1575
