1 NOTE {geni:about_me} http://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Claude,_Duke_of_Aumale
2 CONT
2 CONT Claude of Lorraine, Duke of Aumale (August 18, 1526, Joinville – March 3, 1573, La Rochelle) was the third son of Claude, Duke of Guise and Antoinette de Bourbon. He was a prince of Lorraine by birth.
2 CONT
2 CONT As part of the Treaty of Boulogne which ended the war of the Rough Wooing, Claude, Marquis of Mayenne, was one of six French hostages sent to England. After their father died on 12 April 1550, Claude was allowed to come to Scotlan
2 CONC d, with a passport from Edward VI dated 11 May, to see his sister Mary of Guise and wrote from Edinburgh on 18 May that he would view the strong places of the realm.
2 CONT
2 CONT On August 1, 1547, he married Louise de Brézé (c. 1518 – January 1577), Lady of Anet, the daughter of Louis de Brézé, seigneur d'Anet, and Diane de Poitiers. They had eleven children:
2 CONT
2 CONT  Henri (October 21, 1549, Château de Saint-Germain – August 1559), Count of Valentinois
2 CONT
2 CONT  Catherine Romula (November 8, 1550, Saint-Germain – June 25, 1606), married on May 11, 1569 Nicholas, Duke of Mercœur
2 CONT
2 CONT  Madeleine Diane (b. February 5, 1554), d. young
2 CONT
2 CONT  Charles, Duke of Aumale (1555–1631)
2 CONT
2 CONT  Diane (November 10, 1558 – June 25, 1586, Ligny), married on November 13, 1576 François, Duke of Piney-Luxemburg
2 CONT
2 CONT  Antoinette (b. June 9, 1560, Nancy), d. young
2 CONT
2 CONT  Antoinette Louise (September 29, 1561, Joinville – August 24, 1643, Soissons), Abbess of Soissons
2 CONT
2 CONT  Antoine (b. November 12, 1562), d. young
2 CONT
2 CONT  Claude (December 13, 1564 – January 3, 1591, Saint-Denis), called the "Chevalier d'Aumale", Abbot of St.-Pere-en-Valle, Chartres, Knight of the Order of Malta, General of the Galleys
2 CONT
2 CONT  Charles (January 25, 1566 – May 7, 1568, Paris)
2 CONT
2 CONT  Marie (June 10, 1565 – January 27, 1627), Abbess of Chelles
2 CONT
2 CONT When his brother Francis acceded as Duke of Guise in 1550 he ceded to Claude the title of Duke of Aumale. He was killed by a culverin shot while besieging La Rochelle.
