1 NOTE {geni:about_me} '''Diane de France, Duchesse d’Angoulême (1538–1619)'''
2 CONT
2 CONT Diane de France (also styled Diane de Valois) was the legitimised natural daughter of King Henri II of France and his Italian mistress Filippa Duci. A central court figure across the reigns of five Valois and Bourbon kings (Henri 
2 CONC II through Louis XIII), she was a key political mediator during the French Wars of Religion and a patron of Renaissance architecture.  
2 CONT
2 CONT {{https://www.geni.com/media/proxy?media_id=6000000227510629971&size=large}}
2 CONT ''Portrait de Diane de France, duchesse d'Angoulême (1538-1619), fille légitimée d'Henri II  (c. 1570, Workshop of François Clouet).'' Source:''' "File:Diane de France.jpg." Wikimedia Commons. 26 Apr 2026, 15:58 UTC. <https://comm
2 CONC ons.wikimedia.org/w/index.php?title=File:Diane_de_France.jpg&oldid=1204031035> 30 Aug 2026, 20:40.
2 CONT
2 CONT '''Key Biographical Details'''
2 CONT Full Name & Titles: Diane de France, suo jure Duchesse d’Angoulême (1582), Duchesse de Châtellerault (1563), Duchesse de Castro (by 1st marriage), Duchesse de Montmorency (by 2nd marriage).  
2 CONT Born: July 25, 1538 in Paris (conceived during Henri II’s military campaign in Moncalieri, Piedmont).  
2 CONT Died: January 11, 1619 (aged 80) at the Hôtel d'Angoulême, Paris.
2 CONT Parents: Henri II, King of France (1519–1559) and Filippa Duci (c. 1520–1586). Raised primarily under the care of her aunt, Marguerite de Valois (Duchesse de Berry).  
2 CONT Legitimisation: Formal letters of legitimisation were issued in 1572 under Charles IX (often misdated in older literature to 1547).  
2 CONT Marriages & Issue
2 CONT 1. Orazio Farnese, Duca di Castro (c. 1532–1553)
2 CONT Contract/Marriage: Contract signed February 13, 1552; married February 15, 1553.  
2 CONT Wikipedia
2 CONT Outcome: Widowed 5 months later on July 18, 1553, when Orazio was killed at the Siege of Hesdin. No surviving issue.  
2 CONT 2. François de Montmorency, 2nd Duc de Montmorency (1530–1579)
2 CONT Contract/Marriage: Contract signed May 3, 1557; married June 4, 1557 at the Château de Villers-Cotterêts.  
2 CONT Issue:
2 CONT Anne de Montmorency (born c. September 1560; died young before October 15, 1560).  
2 CONT Outcome: Widowed May 6, 1579. François died without surviving direct male heirs, passing the Montmorency title to his brother Henri I de Montmorency.  
2 CONT Political Role & Architectural Legacy
2 CONT The Politiques Faction: Alongside her second husband, François de Montmorency, Diane was a prominent voice of moderation during the Wars of Religion, advocating for compromise between the Crown and Hugenot leaders.
2 CONT Crown Mediator: Trusted by her half-brother Henri III, she secured the appanage Duchy of Angoulême in 1582. Following Henri III's assassination, she mediated reconciliation between Henri IV and the House of Guise, and later superv
2 CONC ised the education of young Louis XIII.  
2 CONT Hôtel d'Angoulême (Hôtel de Lamoignon): Beginning in 1584, Diane commissioned the grand townhouse in the Marais district of Paris (corner of Rue des Francs-Bourgeois and Rue Pavée), which today houses the Bibliothèque historique d
2 CONC e la Ville de Paris.  
2 CONT
2 CONT {{https://www.geni.com/media/proxy?media_id=6000000227511257828&size=large}}
2 CONT ''Hôtel d'Angoulême (now Hôtel de Lamoignon), Paris.'' Source: M, Alexandre. “Cet Hôtel Particulier Parisien Méconnu Datant De 1589 Cache Une Des Plus Belles Collections De Paris !” Paris ZigZag | Insolite & Secret, 29 Dec. 2023, 
2 CONC https://www.pariszigzag.fr/insolite/histoire-insolite-paris/hotel-de-lamoignon/. Accessed 30 Aug. 2026.
2 CONT
2 CONT Connection to the Montmorency–Halluin Line
2 CONT Through her marriage to François de Montmorency, Diane became closely linked to the main branch of the House of Montmorency. While François and Diane had no surviving children, François' nephew—Charles de Montmorency, Duc de Damvi
2 CONC lle—married Marguerite d'Halluin (marquise de Piennes), binding the Montmorency and Halluin lines in the late 16th century.
2 CONT
2 CONT ----
2 CONT
2 CONT Diane de France (1538 – January 11, 1619) was the natural (illegitimate) daughter of Henry II, King of France, and Filippa Duci, a Piedmontese. Some sources claim that she was the daughter of Diane de Poitiers.
2 CONT
2 CONT Diane was born in Paris during 1538 and was formally legitimised in 1547. She was married in 1553 to Orazio Farnese, Duke of Castro, but was later widowed in the same year after Orazio was killed in battle. Diane's second marriage
2 CONC  was to Francis de Montmorency in 1559, who was the eldest son of Anne, Constable of France. Diane was widowed for a second time in 1579, after helping her to make her husband a leader of the politiques, a moderate Roman Catholic 
2 CONC group in France.
2 CONT
2 CONT Diane became of favourite of Henry III, King of France, who made her Duchess of Angouleme in appanage (during her life time only) during 1582. Diane also enjoyed much respect at the court of Henry IV, King of France and superinten
2 CONC ded the education of his son Louis XIII, King of France.
2 CONT
2 CONT Diane died on January 11, 1619 in Paris. Her surviving letters reveal her as a woman of great courage and tolerance.
2 CONT
2 CONT Source: http://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Diane_de_France
