1 NOTE KING OF POLAND AS SIGISMUND III 1587-1632
1 NOTE "OF POLAND"
1 NOTE Also Sigismund I, of Sweden (1592-99).  He was elected king of Poland by 
2 CONT the Sejm (parliament) in 1587 and succeeded to the Swedish throne on the 
2 CONT death of his father. Unpopular in Protestant Sweden because of his Roman 
2 CONT Catholicism, he was deposed in 1599 and replaced by his uncle, who became 
2 CONT Charles IX. In Poland Sigismund came into frequent conflict with the 
2 CONT szlachta (gentry) and with his chancellor, Jan Zamojski (1542-1605), who 
2 CONT opposed his policy of alliance with Austria. He defeated a revolt by the 
2 CONT gentry in 1606-7 but ultimately failed to wrest power away from the Sejm. 
2 CONT In 1596 he moved the capital from Kraków to Warsaw and won the submission 
2 CONT of his Ukrainian subjects to Roman Catholicism. He tried unsuccessfully to 
2 CONT conquer Russia during the "Time of Troubles" and fought several wars with 
2 CONT the Turks in the 1620s. Sigismund's attempts to regain the throne of 
2 CONT Sweden led to wars with that country which resulted in the loss of most of 
2 CONT Livonia (1629).
