1 NOTE {geni:about_me} Nicholas II, Duke of Lorraine
2 CONT
2 CONT From Wikipedia, the free encyclopedia
2 CONT
2 CONT Nicholas II (December 12, 1612 – January 25, 1670), was Duke of Lorraine and Duke of Bar between 1634 and 1661, when he abdicated for his brother.
2 CONT
2 CONT Nicholas François de Lorraine-Vaudemont was the youngest son of duke Francis II of Lorraine, and as it appeared unlikely that he would succeed to the duchy, he was destined to the church. He was made coadjutor bishop of Toul, in s
2 CONC pite of his youth, in 1619 and succeeded to the see in 1624, but never actually received any sacred orders. He studied at the University of Pont-à-Mousson, 1622—1629 towards doctorates in philosophy and theology. He returned to Na
2 CONC ncy in June 1629. The thesis he prepared on the sacrament of penance was printed in Holland in 1627 with a dedication to Pope Urban VIII. After his promotion to the cardinalate, received private lessons from two Jesuit priests.
2 CONT
2 CONT Nicholas was named abbot in commendam — a lucrative sinecure — of several abbeys and was sent on several embassies by his brother, Charles IV, duke of Lorraine and Bar and by Louis XIII of France.
2 CONT
2 CONT He was created cardinal January 19, 1626, but reserved in pectore and not published until August 30, 1627. He never received the red hat and the cardinal-deaconry. When in 1634 his brother Charles was pressured into resigning his 
2 CONC estates in his favor, and he became duke of Lorraine, he wrote to Urban VIII, March 4, 1634, resigning the cardinalate for having married his cousin Claude Françoise de Lorraine, the second daughter of Henri, duke of Lorraine and 
2 CONC Bar, on February 17-18. On March 8, 1634, the pope declared him in nullo e sacris ordinis constitutum, depriving him of the cardinalate and the administration of the diocese of Toul. In the consistory of March 22, 1634, the pope c
2 CONC ommunicated the news to the cardinals.
2 CONT
2 CONT In 1661 he himself abdicated the throne, making way again for his older brother, Charles IV, Duke of Lorraine.
2 CONT
2 CONT His children: [1]
2 CONT
2 CONT Ferdinand Philippe Joseph François Ignace Dominique Gaspard de Lorraine (1639–1659), styled prince Ferdinand de Lorraine.
2 CONT
2 CONT Charles Léopold Nicolas Sixte de Lorraine (1643–1690), duke of Lorraine and Bar, 1675–1690.
2 CONT
2 CONT Marie Anne (1648-1661)
2 CONT --------------------
