2 CONT
2 CONT [edit] Background
2 CONT
2 CONT She was the eldest daughter of Ferdinand Maria, Elector of Bavaria and his wife Princess Henriette Adelaide of Savoy. Her maternal grandparents were Victor Amadeus I, Duke of Savoy and Christine Marie of France. Christine Marie wa
2 CONC s the second daughter of Henry IV of France and Marie de' Medici.
2 CONT
2 CONT [edit] Biography
2 CONT
2 CONT Born in Munich in modern day Germany, Maria Anna was betrothed to the Dauphin in 1668, at the age of eight, and carefully educated; she could speak several languages, namely her mother tongue German as well as French, Italian and 
2 CONC Latin. She was said to look forward to the fate of becoming Dauphine of France. Maria Anna was very close to her mother who died in 1676. Maria Anna grew up seeing the construction of the Nymphenburg Palace which was began in 1664
2 CONC  after the birth of her brother Maximilian.
2 CONT
2 CONT Her husband was her second cousin. Her siblings included Violante of Bavaria, future wife of Ferdinando de' Medici; as well as the future Maximilian II Emanuel, Elector of Bavaria.
2 CONT
2 CONT Prior to the marriage to Louis, she had a proxy ceremony in Munich on 28 January 1680; the couple would meet for the first time on 7 March 1680 in Châlons-sur-Marne. She was the first Dauphine of France since Mary, Queen of Scots,
2 CONC  who married Francis II of France in 1558.
2 CONT
2 CONT Upon her marriage she took on the rank of her husband which was a Fils de France (Son of France); this meant that she was entitled to the style of Royal Highness and the form of address of Madame la Dauphine.
2 CONT
2 CONT When she arrived in France, she made a good impression with her good French. At her arrival in Strasbourg, she was addressed in German, but interrupted the greeting by saying: "Gentlemen, I speak French!" The impression of her app
2 CONC earance, however, was not as good, and she was called "terribly ugly".
2 CONT
2 CONT Others said, that although she may not have been beautiful, she did have personal charm.
2 CONT
2 CONT From her arrival in France, she was the second most important women at court after her mother in law, the dull Maria Theresa of Spain. The Queen died in July 1683 putting Maria Anna at the top of the hierarchy at Versailles. As su
2 CONC ch, she became the most prominent female at court and was given the apartments of the late Queen. The King expected her to perform the functions of the first lady at court. Her ill health made her unsuitable for such duties, and s
2 CONC he lost the favour of the King, who thought that she failed to do her duty.
2 CONT
2 CONT Her husband took mistresses, and she lived an isolated life in her apartments where she spoke with her friends in German, a language her husband could not understand. She was very close to a fellow German, Elizabeth Charlotte of t
2 CONC he Palatinate; Elizabeth Charlotte was the wife of Philippe, ( who was Louis XIV's younger brother ) and thus sister in law of the King.
2 CONT
2 CONT She was said to suffer that she was not beautiful in a court where beauty was important, which made her depressed. She died in 1690.
2 CONT
2 CONT Her husband predeceased his father. She was buried at the Royal Basilica of Saint Denis. She was the aunt of Joseph Ferdinand of Bavaria, Prince of Asturias (possible candidate for the Spanish throne which her second son later fil
2 CONC led) and Charles VII, Holy Roman Emperor.
2 CONT
2 CONT [edit] Issue
2 CONT
2 CONT     * Louis de France (16 August 1682 – 18 February 1712), Duke of Burgundy and later Dauphin of France; married second cousin Princess Maria Adelaide of Savoy, parents of Louis XV of France;
2 CONT
2 CONT     * Philippe de France (19 December 1683 – 9 July 1746), Duke of Anjou, (later King of Spain); became King of Spain in 1700; married second cousin Princess Maria Luisa Gabriella of Savoy and had issue; married again Elisabeth Fa
2 CONC rnese and had issue such as the future Dauphine of France, Infanta Maria Teresa Rafaela of Spain;
2 CONT
2 CONT     * Charles de France (31 July 1686 – 5 May 1714), Duke of Berry, Alençon and of Angoulême Count of Ponthieu; married his first cousin Marie Louise Élisabeth d'Orléans and had issue but none survived over a year;
2 CONT
