1 NOTE {geni:about_me} *https://it.wikipedia.org/wiki/Manuel_d%27Orl%C3%A9ans,_conte_di_Charny
2 CONT
2 CONT '''Manuel Luis de Orleans and Watteville , II Comte de Charny and I Duke of Castellammare''' (Brussels , 25 of August 1677 - Naples, 14 May 1740)  was a soldier and aristocrat service Philip V of Spain and the Kingdom of Naples . 
2 CONC [ 1 ]
2 CONT
2 CONT
2 CONT ===Biography
2 CONT
2 CONT He was the illegitimate son of Louis of Orleans, Count of Charny, who in turn was the illegitimate son of Gaston of Orleans, brother of Louis XIII of France and son of Henry IV of France. His father had fought on behalf of the kin
2 CONC g of Spain in the war of independence of Portugal, was governor of Oran and died in 1692. In 1698 he entered the Order of Santiago and in 1703 he was appointed master of the field of one of the thirds raised in Extremadura; in 170
2 CONC 6 he ascended to brigadier and participated in the battle of Almansa. In 1707 he was appointed fourth-fourth gentleman, governor of Denia and Infantry inspector of the Kingdoms of Valencia, Murcia and Aragon. In 1711 he was promot
2 CONC ed to field marshal and in 1719 to lieutenant general, at the time he was appointed governor of Jaca. From 1721 to 1722 he was captain general of Aragon. In 1725 he was appointed Governor of Ceuta, a city in which a heraldic shiel
2 CONC d is still seen on the western wall of the fortification called Contraguardia de San Francisco Javier. In 1731 he left Ceuta and was assigned to Italy. He commanded a contingent of 6,000 soldiers who arrived in Liorna on October 2
2 CONC 6, to defend the rights of Charles of Bourbon over the Grand Duchy of Tuscany and the Duchy of Parma. As provided for in Article X of the Treaty of Seville, to allow disembarkation in Florence and on behalf of Spanish troops, he p
2 CONC ublicly swore allegiance to Grand Duke Juan Gastón de' Medici and his heir Carlos, who landed on December 27, 1731. In 1733 the War of the Polish Succession broke out, which will pit Spain against Austria, was appointed lieutenant
2 CONC  general and assumed command of the army sent to conquer the Two Sicilies by Carlos de Borbón, under the orders of Captain General José Carrillo de Albornoz, Duke of Montemar. After the entry of the Spaniards, he led the siege of 
2 CONC Castel SantElmo, which surrendered after five days, on April 25, 1734. After the surrender, with a gesture of military courtesy unusual at this time, he invited the defeated Austrian commander, the Count of Lossada, to dinner with
2 CONC  his officers. In 1737 he was appointed Captain General of Naples, a position he held until his death in May 1740.
2 CONT
2 CONT His ashes are in a small sepulcher in the Pontifical Basilica of San Miguel , in Madrid . In the same chapel there is another tomb with the ashes of his father, who was the one who had the chapel built at his own expense. The ashe
2 CONC s of the father were interred in the basilica in a ceremony that took place on July 13, 1740. Later those of Manuel de Orleans would be inhumed.
2 CONT
2 CONT ===Family
2 CONT
2 CONT ----
2 CONT
2 CONT De sa relation avec Isabel de Watteville il a un fils naturel, Manuel d'Orléans, qui suivra les traces de son père en tant que militaire au service de l'Espagne. Il a également eu une autre fille, une professe du Real Monasterio d
2 CONC e Santa Isabel (en) sous le nom de María Antonia de San Pedro.
2 CONT
2 CONT https://fr.wikipedia.org/wiki/Manuel_d%27Orleans
2 CONT
2 CONT
