1 NOTE {geni:about_me} *'''Sources:'''
2 CONT **Drouin Institute (Archived marriage record - see attached in Media tab)
2 CONT
2 CONT **Geneanet, France, www.geneanet.org
2 CONT
2 CONT 1. René PLOURDEPrint Family Tree Born 15 July 1667 - Vouneuil-sur-Vienne, 86210, Vienne, Nouvelle-Aquitaine, FRANCE Deceased 16 February 1708 - Rivière Ouelle - Kamouraska, Quebec, CANADA , age at death: 40 years old Pionnier en N
2 CONC ouvelle-France
2 CONT
2 CONT Parents François PELOURDE 1639-1671 Périnne GRÉMILLON 1643-1670
2 CONT
2 CONT Spouse Married 26 August 1697, Rivière Quelle, to Jeanne Marguerite BÉRUBÉ 1680-
2 CONT
2 CONT Notes Individual Note ÉMIGRANT QUÉBÉCOIS RÉPERTORIÉ PAR LE PRDH-RAB DE L'UNIVERSITÉ DE MONTRÉAL
2 CONT
2 CONT 2. Godparents at baptism of Rene Plourde, 1667: Jeanne Busseau and Rene Lecomte
2 CONT
2 CONT --------------------
2 CONT === GEDCOM Note ===
2 CONT Rene Plourde, son of Francois and Jeanne Perrine Germillon Plourde, was reported being born on July 10, 1665 in St Pierre, Eveche de Poitiers, France. While Rene Plourde&apos;s birth year had been reported as 1665, it is evident u
2 CONC pon reading a photocopy of the French parish&apos;s baptismal register that the year is 1667. Rene was baptized at his birthplace, Vouneuil-sur-Vienne, Poitou, France, on 15 July 1667. Godfather: Rene Lecomte, Godmother: Jeanne Bu
2 CONC sseau Both of Rene&apos;s parents died sometime before his sister Jeanne Pelourde&apos;s marriage in France in November 1690. Based on undocumented sources, Rene had likely served in the employ of the parish of St. Pierre in Poiti
2 CONC ers, France, prior to his departure for New France in 1692. Poitiers was the principal town of Rene&apos;s Vienne region. The hypothesis is given due to the fact that at the time of Rene Plourde&apos;s marriage contract in 1697, h
2 CONC is parish of origin is noted by the Notary Janneau as being St. Pierre of Poitiers. Baptismal documents and his parent&apos;s marriage records indicate otherwise. Rene, as most people of his time, was illiterate It is interesting 
2 CONC to note that one of the ships transporting settlers to New France, from the port of LaRochelle, was the Jahan. Jeanne-Perrine Gremillon Plourde&apos;s brother, Pierre Gremillon, was married to Jaquette Jahan in 1660. Therefore, Ja
2 CONC quette Jahan Gremillon was Rene Plourde&apos;s maternal aunt by marriage. Such might be the link that encouraged the orphaned Rene to seek adventure, or fortune, as a settler/colonist in New France, and accept recruitment as a dom
2 CONC estic for the Seminary at Quebec. Prior to his departure, Rene, as an employee of the parish of St. Pierre at Poitiers, was signed to a contract for employment by an Agent of the Seminary at Quebec. Such Contractees were subject t
2 CONC o investigation prior to engagement. The usual contract term was for 36 months and served as in-kind payment for passage from France to New France. Rene Plourde left France from LaRochelle in 1692 on the ship Jahan arriving in Nou
2 CONC velle France (New France/Quebec) in the summer of 1692. The next recorded action of our Ancestor, Rene Plourde, is his contract to purchase land in the Seigneurie (or township) of Kamouraska. (Kamouraska is an Amerindian word mean
2 CONC ing &quot; There is rush/bulrush at the water&apos;s edge &quot;.) The French translation of rush is &quot; jonc&quot;. At the Quebec home of the Notary Chambalon, Rene Plourde officially received a land grant for property in the 
2 CONC Seigneurie of Kamouraska. The land was 6 arpents/acres of frontage on the St. Laurent River and 30 arpents deep. Long, narrow properties with river frontage were customary and essential for cultivation during the time of settlemen
2 CONC t in New France. Rene&apos;s property also included a hill at the deepest end. It was summarily called &quot; la montagne a Plourde&quot; or &quot; Plourde&apos;s Mountain &quot;. The feature has been given many names by the Local
2 CONC s, but to this day, official Topographic Maps list the hill with its original name. Between 1694 and 1700, 27 Colonists and their families settled in Kamouraska. The grants in the first year were given to Pierre Emend and Robert P
2 CONC aradis. In 1695, the grantees were Mathurin Dube, Mathurin Ouellet, Pierre Michaud, Sr. and Pierre Michaud, Jr., Pierre Fournier and Rene Plourde. The history of the Kamouraska land grants, specifically, and under the French feuda
2 CONC l system in place at that time, in New France, will not be explained here --- but is available from Rita. While the historical record for Rene Plourde is very sparse, Church records from Notre Dame de Liesse, at Riviere Ouelle ---
2 CONC  the nearest church at the time --- indicate that Rene served as Godfather to Rene Lignet, son of Jean Lignet, an Amerindian, on August18, 1695. Also the Registers of Notre Dame de Liesse record that Rene Plourde served as witness
2 CONC  to the marriage of Charles Miville and Louise Grondine on February 13, 1697. Such would indicate that Rene was known to his future bride&apos;s family, before their nuptials in August 1697. Rene&apos;s exact date of death in 1708
2 CONC  is not recorded, but one can be certain that it occurred shortly before Jeanne Sauvenier Berube Soucy Miville&apos;s (mother of Jeanne Berube Plourde) testament regarding the inheritance of property for her heirs from three marri
2 CONC ages, and the guardianship of her minor children, dated November 15 1708. Document prepared by the Notary Janneau. Rene, having been 30 years old at his marriage, died at about 41 years. Surviving were his Spouse and six young chi
2 CONC ldren. The land grant that had originally been noted as being 6 arpents/acres of river frontage by 30 arpents/acres deep was recorded in 1724 as being 4 arpents/acres of river frontage by 42 arpents/acres deep. Also, the original 
2 CONC land grant was within the Seigneurie of C/Kamouraska, yet in 1724, it is listed as being situated in the Seigneurie of La Bouteillerie. Since Renee Plourde was the only immigrant with the patronym (Plourde), the surname was transm
2 CONC itted, to this day, by Renee and Jeanne-Marguerite&apos;s sons, Pierre, with 11 children and Augustin with 14 children. The third son, Joseph, died without posterity. In the patrilineal, patri-focused society, daughter, Marie Cath
2 CONC erine Plourde&apos;s children would be listed as descendants of their father, Rene Dube. Their matrilineal ancestry would not be considered for registry.
2 CONT
2 CONT Source (http://www.lafreniere.info/ged/n_b.htm) _COLOR 3 _AMTID 400129280395:1030:114438726 _COLOR 3
