1 OCCU Soldier with 1774 de Anza Expedition
1 NOTE {geni:about_me} Served as a Spanish soldier garrisoned at Monterey in 1782.
2 CONT
2 CONT From the 1790 census, Ygnacio Cantua was born in Navojoa, Sonora, New Spain. Since 1614, along the Rio Mayo the Jesuits had consolidated several rancherias (Native Indian hamlets) around seven Mission settlements, the three most i
2 CONC mportant being the Mission Pueblos of Navojoa, Tesia, and, near the coast, Santa Cruz de Mayo. Each of these cabeceras (church centers) contained two to three thousand people with a resident priest, and became the sacred ceremonia
2 CONC l center for the local Mayo people. (The four smaller pueblos, visitas, were affiliated with one of the cabeceras and tended by a missionary.) This cabecera/visita organization was the primary method used by the Jesuits for evange
2 CONC lizing the Native Peoples for their time in New Spain, from 1565 to their expulsion in 1767.
2 CONT
2 CONT Sources: Polzer, C and Sheridan, T. ''The Presidio and Militia on the Northern Frontier of New Spain: Part  The Californias and Sinaloa-Sonora, 1700-1765.'' University of Arizona Press. 1997. Page 363
2 CONT
2 CONT West, Robert C. ''Sonora: Its Geographical Personality.'' University of Texas Press. 2010. Chapter 3, map
