0 @N6000000001956352061@ NOTE
1 CONT Second Duke of Epernon.
1 CONT Bernard de Nogaret de La Valette (1592-1661), duke of Épernon and a French general, was the son of Jean Louis de Nogaret de La Valette and Marguerite de Foix-Candale, grand-daughter of the constable of Montmorency. Through his mot
1 CONC her's line, Bernard could also claim the English title of Duke of Kendal, originally granted to his ancestor John de Foix in 1446.
1 CONT In 1622, Bernard married Gabrielle-Angélique de Verneuil, legitimised daughter of Henri IV and the Marquise de Verneuil, with whom he had a son, Louis-Charles-Gaston de Candale, and a daughter, Anne-Louise-Christine de Foix de La 
1 CONC Valette d'Épernon. Gabrielle-Angélique died in 1627 (some say Bernard poisoned her) and in 1634 he married Marie Ducambaut, a niece of Cardinal Richelieu. It was not a happy marriage, as Bernard later conceived a life-long passion
1 CONC  for a middle-class woman named Ninon de Lartigue, who exerted absolute power over him and to whom he gave enormous sums of money.
1 CONT Like his father, Bernard made a career of the military. He was named Colonel-General of infantry and fought at the sieges of Saint-Jean-d'Angély and of Royan (1621) and at the attack of the pas de Suse (1629). On 15 May 1633, Bern
1 CONC ard became a Chevalier du Saint-Esprit and in 1635 he was charged by Louis XIII with restoring the order which had been disturbed by lifting of taxes and religious passions. He fought in Picardy (1636), in Guyenne, and finally aga
1 CONC inst the Spaniards, and repressed the Peasants' Revolt (Révolte des Croquants) in 1637.
0 @N6000000001212169742@ NOTE Notes 2002:  From the data of Renee Adams on RootsWeb.com's WorldConnect Project:
1 CONT Note:
1 CONT From Jim White:
1 CONT Fled France when Cardinal Richelieu accused him of complicity with the enemy at the battle of Fontarable (1636); first to the Netherlands, then to Glamorganshire, Wales.
1 CONT Heir to Duc d'Epernon (since elder brother inherited from mother). Named after his uncle, the Duc d'Epernon's brother.
1 CONT Executedin effigy 6 Jun 1639; returned to France and rehabilitated 1643.
1 CONT Mouton (Epernon of Old France) is unaware of any Alice __ or children born out of France.
1 CONT
1 CONT One source: Tapie, France in the Age of Louis XIII and Richelieu
1 CONT He fled tothe Netherlands with Alice (Antoinette), the wife who bore him John d'Epernon (Epperson). John later came to America.
