0 @N6000000000551129829@ NOTE Henrietta Maria de Bourbon (F) b. 26 November 1609, d. 21 August 1669, #101384d. 21 Aug 1669|p10139.htm#i101384|Henri IV, Roi de France|b. 13 Dec 1553d. 14 May 1610|p10139.htm#i101385|Marie de Medici|b. 26 Apr 1573d. 3 Jul 1642|p1
1 CONC 0139.htm#i101386|Antoine de Bourbon, Roi de Navarre|b. 22 Apr 1518d. 17 Nov 1562|p10186.htm#i101857|Jeanne I. d\\'Albret, Reine de Navarre|b. 7 Jan 1528d. 9 Jun 1572|p11366.htm#i113659|Francesco I. de Medici, Granduca di Toscana|b
1 CONC . 25 Mar 1541d. 19 Oct 1587|p11204.htm#i112038|Joanna Erzherzögin von Österreich|b. 1547d. 1578|p11204.htm#i112037|');"Pedigree <JavaScript:pup('Henrietta Maria de Bourbon|b. 26 Nov 1609<BR> Last Edited=5 Jun 2003 Consanguinity In
1 CONC dex=0.1%
1 CONT Henrietta Maria de Bourbon was the daughter of Henri IV, Roi de France and Marie de Medici. She was born on 26 November 1609 at Hotel du Louvre, Paris, France.1 She married Charles I Stuart, King of Great Britain <p10138.htm>, son
1 CONC  of James I Charles Stuart, King of Great Britain <p10137.htm> and Anne Oldenburg, Princess of Denmark <p10138.htm>, on 13 June 1625 at St. Augustine's Church, Canterbury, Kent, England, Married by proxy earlier, on 1 May, in Pari
1 CONC s.1 She died on 21 August 1669 at age 59 at Château of St. Colombes, Colombe, Ile de France, France.1 She was also reported to have died on 10 September 1669 at Colombe, Paris, France. She was buried at Cathedral of St. Denis, St.
1 CONC  Denis, Ile de France, France.1
1 CONT Children of Henrietta Maria de Bourbon and Charles I Stuart, King of Great Britain <p10138.htm>:
1 CONT Charles James Stuart, Duke of Cornwall b. 13 May 1629, d. 13 May 1629 Charles II Stuart, King of Great Britain+ b. 29 May 1630, d. 6 Feb 1685 Mary Henrietta Stuart, Princess Royal of Great Britain+ b. 4 Nov 1631, d. 24 Dec 1660 Ja
1 CONC mes II Stuart, King of Great Britain <p10136.htm>+ b. 14 Oct 1633, d. 16 Sep 1701 Elizabeth Stuart b. 29 Dec 1635, d. 8 Sep 1650 Anne Stuart <p10140.htm> b. 17 Mar 1637, d. 5 Nov 1640 Catherine Stuart <p10140.htm> b. 29 Jun 1639, 
1 CONC d. 29 Jun 1639 Henry Stuart, Duke of Gloucester <p10140.htm> b. 8 Jul 1640, d. 13 Sep 1660 Henrietta Anne Stuart <p10140.htm>+ b. 16 Jun 1644, d. 30 Jun 1670
1 CONT Citations
1 CONT [S11 <s1.htm>] Alison Weir, Britain's Royal Family: A Complete Genealogy (London, U.K.: The Bodley Head, 1999), page 252. Hereinafter cited as Britain's Royal Family.
1 CONT
1 CONT Queen Henrietta Maria Bourbon was born on 26 Nov 1609 in Hotel du Louvre (Paris), Ile de France, France. She was buried in 1669 in Royal Basilica of St. Denis, Paris, France. She died on 31 Aug 1669 in Colombes (near Paris), Ile d
1 CONC e France, France. Parents: King Henry IV Bourbon of France  and Marie de Medici
1 CONT She was married to King Charles I Stuart of England  on 13 Jun 1625 in Canterbury Cathedral, Canterbury, Kent, England. Children were: Charles James Stewart , King Charles II Stuart of England , Princess Mary Stuart of Orange , Ki
1 CONC ng James II Stuart of England , Elizabeth Stewart , Anne Stewart , Catherine Stewart , Duke Henry Stewart of Gloucester , Duchess Henrietta Anne Stewart of Orleans .
1 CONT
1 CONT Henrietta Maria (1609-69), the youngest daughter of Henry IV of France and Marie de Medici. She married Charles I of England, who agreed, in the marriage contract, to relieve the English Catholics, but failed to effect the Queens 
1 CONC wishes. Between 1629 and 1639 she opposed Lauds proclamation against Roman Catholic recusants, and raised money for the royal cause in the bishops wars (1639). She encouraged the army plot, attempted to save Strafford, and urged C
1 CONC harles to arrest the five members. From 1644 to 1660 she lived in France. See Taylors Life of Queen Henrietta Maria (1905). [World Wide Illustrated Encyclopedia, 1935]
1 CONT
1 CONT Henrietta Maria (1609-69), queen consort of England, the wife of King Charles I and mother of Charles II. She was the daughter of Henry IV of France and Marie de Médicis. Henrietta Marias brother, Louis XIII, king of France, conse
1 CONC nted in 1624 to her marriage to Charles, then Prince of Wales, on the condition that the English laws directed against Roman Catholics be revised; the marriage took place, but the laws were not revised. Because Henrietta Maria eng
1 CONC aged in political intrigue to aid the Roman Catholic cause, the queen greatly increased the unpopularity of her husband. In 1642, after the outbreak of the English Revolution, she went to the Continent to secure assistance for the
1 CONC  Royalists. The following year she returned with money and joined Charles at Oxford. The situation grew worse for the Royalists, however, and in 1644 Henrietta Maria fled to France. She continued to solicit aid for Charles until h
1 CONC is execution in 1649. After the restoration of Charles II in 1660 she was awarded a parliamentary grant and permission to live in England; she returned to France in 1665. [Microsoft Encarta 98 Encyclopedia]
