0 @N6000000000551192063@ NOTE James I Charles Stuart, King of Great Britain1 (M) b. 19 June 1566, d. 27 March 1625, #101370d. 27 Mar 1625|p10137.htm#i101370|Henry Stuart, Lord Darnley|b. 7 Dec 1545d. 10 Feb 1567|p10190.htm#i101891|Mary Stewart, Queen of Scotla
1 CONC nd|b. 7 Dec 1542d. 8 Feb 1587|p10189.htm#i101889|Matthew Stuart, 4th Earl of Lennox|b. 21 Sep 1516d. 4 Sep 1571|p10208.htm#i102074|Lady Margaret Douglas|b. 18 Oct 1515d. 9 Mar 1578|p10208.htm#i102073|James V. Stewart, King of Scot
1 CONC land|b. 15 Apr 1512d. 14 Dec 1542|p10148.htm#i101480|Marie de Guise|b. 22 Nov 1515d. 10 Jun 1560|p10190.htm#i101893|');"Pedigree <JavaScript:pup('James I Charles Stuart, King of Great Britain|b. 19 Jun 1566<BR> Last Edited=22 May 
1 CONC 2004 Consanguinity Index=3.7%
1 CONT James I Charles Stuart, King of Great Britain was the son of Henry Stuart, Lord Darnley <p10190.htm> and Mary Stewart, Queen of Scotland <p10189.htm>.2 He was born on 19 June 1566 at Edinburgh Castle, Edinburgh, Edinburgh, Scotlan
1 CONC d.1 He married Anne Oldenburg, Princess of Denmark <p10138.htm>, daughter of Frederik II Oldenburg, King of Denmark <p10139.htm> and Sophia von Mecklenburg-Güstrow <p10139.htm>, on 23 November 1589 at Oslo, Norway, in a, and again
1 CONC  on 21 Jan 1590 at Kronberg Castle, Copenhagen, Denmark marriage.3 He died on 27 March 1625 at age 58 at Theobalds Park, Hertfordshire, England, from kidney failure.4 He was buried at Westminster Abbey, Westminster, London, Englan
1 CONC d.4  He gained the title of Duke of Rothesay on 19 June 1566.1 He gained the title of Prince James of Scotland on 19 June 1566.2 He succeeded to the title of 2nd Earl of Ross [S., 1565] on 10 February 1567.1 He succeeded to the ti
1 CONC tle of 2nd Lord Ardmannoch [S., 1565] on 10 February 1567.1 He succeeded to the title of 2nd Duke of Albany [S., 1565] on 10 February 1567.2 He succeeded to the title of King James VI of Scotland on 24 July 1567.1 He was crowned K
1 CONC ing of Scotland on 29 July 1567 at Church of the Holy Rood, Stirling, Stirlingshire, Scotland.1 He was godfather for James Elphinstone, 1st Lord Coupar <p7276.htm> at his baptism.5 He succeeded to the title of King James I of Grea
1 CONC t Britain on 24 March 1603.6 He was crowned King of England, Scotland, France and Ireland, Defender of the Faith on 25 July 1603 at Westminster Abbey, Westminster, London.6 He was godfather for Sir James Howard, Lord Maltravers <p
1 CONC 3007.htm> at his baptism on 17 July 1607.7 He was godfather for James Murray, 2nd Earl of Tullibardine <p2216.htm> at his baptism on 22 September 1617 at Cheshunt, Hertfordshire, England.8  Although James married and had seven chi
1 CONC ldren, by nature he was homosexual and the appointment of favourites as advisers caused resentment. James had a Presbyterian background and the Roman Catholic conspirators who attempted to blow up the opening of Parliament on Tues
1 CONC day 5 November 1605 were suitably dealt with. Robert Cecil and Francis Bacon were among his advisers but he had Sir Walter Raleigh executed for fighting in Spain's American colonies and would not tolerate privateering which had be
1 CONC en so profitable in Elizabeth's reign. James's faculties declined as he grew older. He has an extensive biography in the Dictionary of National Biography.9  <e74.htm> <e74.htm>
1 CONT Children of James I Charles Stuart, King of Great Britain and Anne Oldenburg, Princess of Denmark <p10138.htm>:
1 CONT Henry Frederick Stuart, Prince of Wales <p10138.htm> b. 19 Feb 1594, d. 6 Nov 1612 child Stuart <p10502.htm> b. Jul 1595, d. Jul 1595 Elizabeth Stuart, Princess of England <p10138.htm>+ b. 19 Aug 1596, d. 13 Feb 1662 Margaret Stua
1 CONC rt <p10138.htm> b. 24 Dec 1598, d. Mar 1600 Charles I Stuart, King of Great Britain <p10138.htm>+ b. 19 Nov 1600, d. 30 Jan 1649 Robert Bruce Stuart, Duke of Kintyre <p10138.htm> b. 18 Jan 1602, d. 27 May 1602 Son Stuart <p10138.h
1 CONC tm> b. May 1603, d. May 1603 Mary Stuart <p10138.htm> b. 8 Apr 1605, d. 16 Sep 1607 Sophia Stuart <p10138.htm> b. 22 Jun 1606, d. 23 Jun 1606
1 CONT Citations
