0 @N6000000002188028330@ NOTE James I of England
1 CONT From Wikipedia, the free encyclopedia
1 CONT
1 CONT King James VI and I 
1 CONT
1 CONT King of England, King of Scots, and King of Ireland 
1 CONT Reign 24 March 1603 - 27 March 1625 
1 CONT Born June 19, 1566 
1 CONT  Edinburgh Castle 
1 CONT Died March 27, 1625 
1 CONT Predecessor Mary, Queen of Scots (Scotland)
1 CONT Elizabeth I (England) 
1 CONT Successor Charles I 
1 CONT Consort Anne of Denmark 
1 CONT Issue Henry Frederick, Elizabeth of Bohemia, Margaret Stuart, Charles I, Robert Stuart 
1 CONT Royal House Stuart 
1 CONT Father Lord Darnley 
1 CONT Mother Mary, Queen of Scots 
1 CONT James VI of Scotland/James I of England and Ireland (Charles James Stuart) (June 19, 1566 – March 27, 1625) was King of Scots, King of England, and King of Ireland and was the first to style himself King of Great Britain. He ruled
1 CONC  in Scotland as James VI from 24 July 1567. Then from the 'Union of the Crowns', in England and Ireland as James I, from 24 March 1603 until his death. He was the first monarch of England from the House of Stuart, succeeding the l
1 CONC ast Tudor monarch, Elizabeth I, who died without issue.
1 CONT
1 CONT Contents [hide]
1 CONT 1 Summary 
1 CONT 2 Childhood as King James VI of Scotland 
1 CONT 2.1 Birth 
1 CONT 2.2 Regencies 
1 CONT 3 Path to the Union of the Crowns 
1 CONT 3.1 Relationship with Elizabeth I 
1 CONT 3.2 Marriage 
1 CONT 3.3 Witch trials and Sodomy Act 
1 CONT 3.4 Proclaimed James I of England 
1 CONT 4 Early reign in England 
1 CONT 4.1 Political challenges 
1 CONT 4.2 Conflicts with Parliament 
1 CONT 4.3 The Addled Parliament 
1 CONT 4.4 Religious challenges 
1 CONT 4.5 Relationships with Roman Catholicism 
1 CONT 4.6 Gunpowder, treason and plot 
1 CONT 5 Later years 
1 CONT 5.1 Continuing problems with Parliament 
1 CONT 5.2 Thirty Years' War 
1 CONT 5.3 Relationship with Spain 
1 CONT 5.4 The Church in Scotland 
1 CONT 6 Personal relationships 
1 CONT 7 Deaths 
1 CONT 8 Legacy 
1 CONT 8.1 Historical 
1 CONT 8.2 Religious and literary 
1 CONT 8.3 Geographical 
1 CONT 9 Criticism and revisionism 
1 CONT 10 Style and arms 
1 CONT 11 Issue 
1 CONT 12 References 
1 CONT 13 Sources 
1 CONT 14 External links 
1 CONT 15 See also 
1 CONT
1 CONT [edit]
1 CONT Summary
1 CONT James was a successful monarch in Scotland, but the same was not true in England. He was unable to deal with a hostile Parliament and the refusal on the part of the House of Commons to impose sufficiently high taxes crippled the r
1 CONC oyal finances. His taste for political absolutism, his mismanagement of the kingdom's funds and his cultivation of unpopular favourites established the foundation for the English Civil War, during which James' son and successor, C
1 CONC harles I, was tried and executed. During James' own life, however, the governments of the Kingdom of England and the Kingdom of Scotland were relatively stable.
1 CONT
1 CONT Along with Alfred the Great, James is considered to have been one of the most intellectual and learned individuals ever to sit on the English or Scottish thrones. Under him, much of the cultural flourishing of Elizabethan England 
1 CONC continued; science, literature and art, contributed by individuals such as Sir Francis Bacon and William Shakespeare grew by leaps and bounds during his reign. James himself was a talented scholar, writing works such as Daemonolog
1 CONC ie (1597), The True Law of Free Monarchies (1598), Basilikon Doron (1599) and A Counterblaste to Tobacco (1604). King James was known by the epithet “the wisest fool in Christendom’’.
1 CONT
1 CONT [edit]
1 CONT Childhood as King James VI of Scotland
1 CONT [edit]
1 CONT Birth
1 CONT James was the only child of Mary I, Queen of Scots and of her second husband, Henry Stuart, Duke of Albany, more commonly known as Lord Darnley. Both of James's parents were descendants of Henry VII through his great-grandmother M
1 CONC argaret Tudor, sister of Henry VIII. James' mother was an insecure ruler, as both she and her husband, being Roman Catholics, faced a rebellion of Protestant noblemen. Their marriage, furthermore, was a particularly difficult one.
1 CONC  While Mary was pregnant with James, Lord Darnley murdered the Queen's private secretary, David Rizzio. He was dragged outside, even as he held onto the Queens skirt , and brutally murdered.[citation needed]
1 CONT
1 CONT James was born on 19 June 1566 at Edinburgh Castle, and as the eldest son of the monarch and heir-apparent, automatically became Duke of Rothesay and Prince and Great Steward of Scotland. He received the name Charles James, the fi
1 CONC rst name in honour of his godfather Charles IX of France, thus becoming the first British monarch to have more than one forename.
1 CONT
1 CONT James' father was murdered on 10 February 1567 at the Hamiltons' house, Kirk o' Field, Edinburgh,. Mary's marriage on 15 May of the same year to James Hepburn, 4th Earl of Bothwell, who was widely suspected of murdering the Duke o
1 CONC f Albany, contributed further to her unpopularity. In June 1567, the Protestant rebels arrested Mary and imprisoned her in Loch Leven Castle. Mary was forced to abdicate the throne on 24 July, in favour of James, who was still a b
1 CONC aby.
1 CONT
1 CONT British Royalty 
1 CONT House of Stuart 
1 CONT James VI & I 
1 CONT Children include 
1 CONT    Henry, Prince of Wales 
1 CONT    Elizabeth, Queen of Bohemia 
1 CONT    Charles I 
1 CONT    Robert, Duke of Kintyre 
1 CONT Charles I 
1 CONT Children 
1 CONT    Charles II 
1 CONT    James II & VII 
1 CONT    Henry, Duke of Gloucester 
1 CONT    Mary, Princess Royal 
1 CONT    Henrietta, Duchesse d'Orléans 
1 CONT Charles II 
1 CONT James II & VII 
1 CONT Children 
1 CONT    Mary II 
1 CONT    Anne 
1 CONT    James, The Old Pretender 
1 CONT Grandchildren 
1 CONT    Bonnie Prince Charles, The Old Pretender 
