0 @N6000000005599175971@ NOTE Sophia of Hanover From Wikipedia, the free encyclopedia.
1 CONT The Electress Sophia of Hanover was born Sophia, Pfalzgräfin von Simmern, at The Hague on October 14, 1630, and died at Herrenhausen on June 8, 1714. Daughter of Frederick V, Elector Palatine also known as King Frederick V of Bohe
1 CONC mia and Elizabeth Stuart also known as Queen Elizabeth of Bohemia.
1 CONT
1 CONT She married Ernst August, Duke of Brunswick-Lüneburg at Heidelberg September 30, 1658. He became the Elector Holy_Roman_Empire of Hanover in 1692. (Electors were princes who had the right to vote to elect the emperor of the Holy R
1 CONC oman Empire.)
1 CONT
1 CONT As the daughter of Elizabeth Stuart, in turn the daughter of James I & VI /James I of England and Scotland, she was inserted into the line of succession to the British throne behind Queen Anne/Anne I of the United_Kingdom, as her 
1 CONC closest Protestant  heir, by the Act of Settlement of 1701, for the purpose of cutting off any claim by the Catholic James Francis Edward Stuart, who would otherwise have become King James III, as well as denying the throne to man
1 CONC y other Catholics who held a claim. Sophia would have inherited the throne if she had not died before Anne. Upon her death, Sophia's son Elector Georg Ludwig of Hanover/George_I_of_Great_Britain became heir-presumptive, and weeks 
1 CONC later succeeded Queen Anne as King George I of Great Britain.
1 CONT
1 CONT The Act of Settlement restricts the throne to the "Protestant heirs" of Sophia of Hanover who have never been Catholic and who have never married a Catholic. The Royal Marriages Act 1772  also provides that, with certain exception
1 CONC s, they must apply for permission from the British monarch for any marriage they make. Presently there are almost 5000 descendants of Sophia although not all are in the line of succession. The Sophia Naturalization Act of 1705 gra
1 CONC nted the right of British citizenship to Sophia's non-Catholic descendants (though this has been modified by subsequent laws).
1 CONT
1 CONT Before her marriage, Sophia, as the daughter of Frederick V, Elector Palatine of the Rhine, was referred to as Sophie, Princess Palatine of the Rhine, or as Sophia of the Palatinate.
